1
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:06,951
On October second, this low-water crossing near Fort Hood,

2
00:00:06,951 --> 00:00:09,911
Texas, went from dry to more than seven feet under

3
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:11,990
water in about six hours.

4
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:15,229
Over half of flood drownings happen when someone drives into

5
00:00:15,229 --> 00:00:16,552
water like this.

6
00:00:16,552 --> 00:00:18,955
RiverWatch watches crossings like this one,

7
00:00:18,955 --> 00:00:21,979
through cameras that already exist.

8
00:00:22,279 --> 00:00:23,034
The U.S.

9
00:00:23,034 --> 00:00:27,202
Geological Survey runs more than thirteen hundred river cameras,

10
00:00:27,202 --> 00:00:29,535
and nobody measures their images.

11
00:00:29,535 --> 00:00:32,113
Gages fail exactly when they matter.

12
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:34,968
Right now, the official gage at this crossing has a

13
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:38,266
silent primary sensor and a backup stuck at six point

14
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:41,812
two feet, while the camera shows a dry creek.

15
00:00:42,112 --> 00:00:45,560
Everything visual is classical OpenCV 5,

16
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:47,336
with no trained network.

17
00:00:47,336 --> 00:00:50,924
A quality gate rejects infrared night frames,

18
00:00:50,924 --> 00:00:52,422
blur and fog.

19
00:00:52,422 --> 00:00:57,170
ECC registration aligns each frame using only the bridge and

20
00:00:57,170 --> 00:00:58,343
the banks.

21
00:00:58,343 --> 00:01:02,963
A dense normalized cross-correlation between frames fifteen minutes apart shows

22
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:03,985
what moved:

23
00:01:03,985 --> 00:01:05,831
flowing water changes everywhere,

24
00:01:05,831 --> 00:01:07,445
concrete does not.

25
00:01:07,445 --> 00:01:10,208
Each water body must be dense and smooth,

26
00:01:10,208 --> 00:01:13,389
which rejects sunlit gravel under a moving shadow.

27
00:01:13,389 --> 00:01:15,874
The top edge of the water across the channel becomes

28
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:17,522
a virtual staff gauge,

29
00:01:17,522 --> 00:01:20,314
calibrated against the official gage.

30
00:01:20,645 --> 00:01:25,034
We evaluated on almost four thousand frames with cross-validation by

31
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:28,447
day, so the flood day was never used for calibration.

32
00:01:28,447 --> 00:01:31,918
When water flows, the camera is within twenty centimetres on

33
00:01:31,918 --> 00:01:36,133
average, and eighty-seven percent of readings are within one foot.

34
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:37,561
For the question that matters,

35
00:01:37,561 --> 00:01:39,813
is the crossing under a foot of water,

36
00:01:39,813 --> 00:01:42,872
precision is ninety-seven percent.

37
00:01:43,179 --> 00:01:45,431
RiverWatch also looks ahead.

38
00:01:45,431 --> 00:01:49,112
A small forecast, trained on nine and a half years

39
00:01:49,112 --> 00:01:52,931
of both gages, predicts whether the crossing will close within

40
00:01:52,931 --> 00:01:54,116
the next hour.

41
00:01:54,116 --> 00:01:57,251
On October second, the upstream gage jumped at ten in

42
00:01:57,251 --> 00:02:00,583
the morning, and the warning fired twenty-five minutes before the

43
00:02:00,583 --> 00:02:02,150
water covered the crossing.

44
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:05,215
Tested year by year on data it never saw,

45
00:02:05,215 --> 00:02:08,617
it warned before thirty-two of forty-six closures,

46
00:02:08,617 --> 00:02:11,397
most of them at least half an hour ahead.

47
00:02:11,712 --> 00:02:15,149
An Amazon Nova agent on Bedrock decides what to do

48
00:02:15,149 --> 00:02:18,411
next. This is a replay of October second on a

49
00:02:18,411 --> 00:02:19,932
simulated clock.

50
00:02:19,932 --> 00:02:22,811
At nine in the morning the forecast warns,

51
00:02:22,811 --> 00:02:26,434
and the agent sends the duty operator an early caution,

52
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:29,383
an hour and a half before the water arrives.

53
00:02:29,383 --> 00:02:32,262
At ten twenty-five the water covers the crossing.

54
00:02:32,262 --> 00:02:34,456
The agent asks to close it,

55
00:02:34,456 --> 00:02:37,556
but a guardrail in code blocks the request:

56
00:02:37,556 --> 00:02:40,818
escalation needs two independent readings.

57
00:02:40,818 --> 00:02:44,359
Fifteen minutes later, with camera and gage agreeing,

58
00:02:44,359 --> 00:02:47,192
it asks the operator to close the crossing.

59
00:02:47,192 --> 00:02:49,851
Nothing reaches the public without a human.

60
00:02:49,851 --> 00:02:51,592
When the camera misses water,

61
00:02:51,592 --> 00:02:54,309
the agent looks at the frame itself and flags the

62
00:02:54,309 --> 00:02:55,487
camera.

63
00:02:55,787 --> 00:02:58,132
It runs live on AWS.

64
00:02:58,132 --> 00:03:02,683
A Lambda function on Graviton runs OpenCV and the agent

65
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:07,142
every fifteen minutes, S3 and CloudFront serve this page,

66
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:09,847
and SNS notifies the operator.

67
00:03:09,847 --> 00:03:12,725
Today it reports the crossing as dry and the official

68
00:03:12,725 --> 00:03:17,224
gage as broken, instead of raising a false alarm.

69
00:03:17,524 --> 00:03:19,614
It works on one camera so far,

70
00:03:19,614 --> 00:03:22,853
in daylight, with a quick calibration per site.

71
00:03:22,853 --> 00:03:24,594
The limits are in the report.

72
00:03:24,594 --> 00:03:26,684
But the cameras are already there.

73
00:03:26,684 --> 00:03:29,656
RiverWatch turns them into a second pair of eyes for

74
00:03:29,656 --> 00:03:31,206
every crossing.

